<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Patrice de MacMahon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1808">1808</a>–<a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Patrice de MacMahon was a French marshal of Irish descent and president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1873 to 1879. He won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magenta">Battle of Magenta</a> in 1859, was captured with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> at Sedan in 1870 and crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a> in 1871. As president he arbitrated the dispute between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Portugal over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a>, awarding it to Portugal in 1875.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/patrice-de-macmahon.jpg" alt="Patrice de MacMahon" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathieu Deroche · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Patrice_de_MacMahon_Photograph_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The MacMahon family is of Irish origin. After losing much of its land in the Cromwellian confiscations of 1652, a branch supported the deposed James II in 1688 and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, applying for French citizenship in 1749. Patrice de MacMahon was born on 13 June 1808 at the Château de Sully near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Autun</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, the sixteenth and second-youngest son of Baron Maurice-François de Mac Mahon. The family, a military one, had decidedly royalist politics. He entered the military school at Saint-Cyr on 23 October 1825 and joined the army in 1827.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>MacMahon served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-conquest-of-algeria">French conquest of Algeria</a> from 1830, was commended at the seizure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> and was wounded at the Siege of Constantine in 1837. He became a colonel in December 1845 and a general of brigade in 1848, and in 1852 he organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> the plebiscite that approved the coup d'état of 1851. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> he won a victory at the Battle of Malakoff in September 1855, where he is reputed to have said 'Here I am; here will I stay!' At Magenta in 1859 he advanced his troops without orders at a critical moment, which assured the French victory; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> made him a Marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Duke of Magenta.</p><p>He was Governor General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> from 1864, a post in which he did not distinguish himself and which drew numerous complaints. In 1870 he led the main French army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>, suffered defeats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and was seriously wounded at Sedan; with the Emperor he was made prisoner on 1 September. In May 1871 he led the Semaine Sanglante, the week-long campaign that defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-commune">Paris Commune</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In May 1873 the royalist and conservative majority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> elected MacMahon President, with one vote against. Many hoped he would hold office until a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> pretender could restore the monarchy, but he refused to meet the Count of Chambord and accepted a seven-year mandate on 9 November 1873. He remained above parties during the Constitutional Laws of 1875. After the Republican victory of 1876 he reluctantly accepted governments under Jules Dufaure and Jules Simon.</p><p>In the crisis of 16 May 1877 he dismissed Simon, had the Senate dissolve the Chamber of Deputies and campaigned across the country, but the elections of 14 October gave the Republicans a majority of 120 seats. He resigned on 30 January 1879, succeeded by Jules Grévy. He died on 17 October 1893 and was buried at the Invalides after a state funeral.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Patrice de MacMahon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Paris, workers and National Guards rose and took power as the Paris Commune, which the army under <b>Patrice de MacMahon</b> crushed in the Bloody Week of May 1871.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the French Third Republic, after President <b>Patrice de MacMahon</b> failed to impose a government on the Chamber of Deputies in 1877, ministries depended wholly on shifting majorities.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The 1875 <b>MacMahon</b> arbitration awarded Delagoa Bay to Portugal.</blockquote><a href="/state/tembe-kingdom">Tembe Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Château de Sully, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Montcresson, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.01,4.47&amp;year=1863" aria-label="Château de Sully on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Patrice de MacMahon"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5261 6093h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5261 6093h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.01,4.47&amp;year=1863">What was here in 1863? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
